 2. Identify the role of each citation in the paragraph:

    The paragraph text (with line numbers for context):
      "Soon after the necklace splitting problem was originally introduced [ 6], Goldberg and West [ 9] 88 proved that a solution always exists if k= 2. Alon and West [ 3] gave a simpler proof using 89 the Borsuk–Ulam theorem [ 7], and Alon [ 1] generalized the results to k>2. Finally, Alon 90 and Graur [ 2] discovered an efficient approximation algorithm for finding a solution with few 91 cuts. Alon and Graur [ 2] also consider an online variation of the problem. They derive lower 92 and upper bounds on the number of cuts needed when k= 2based on the value of n. They 93 also generalize some of these results to the k>2case. 94 In addition to necklace splitting, other fair division problems can be extended to dynamic 95 settings. Kash et al. [ 13] study a fair division problem with divisible goods where agents with 96 Leontiefpreferencesarriveovertimeandgoodsmustbeirrevocablyallocated. Benadeetal.[ 5] 97 study the problem of allocating indivisible goods that arrive in an online manner, again with 98 irrevocable decisions. He et al. [ 11] generalize this to a setting where reallocation is allowed 99 but expensive. 100 ICDT 2026"

 3. We have 9 citations (from 1 to 9) with their context.

    Let's map each citation to the paragraph:

      Citation 1: [6] -> "Soon after the necklace splitting problem was originally introduced [ 6]"
          Role: Introduces the original problem. This is the starting point of the history.

      Citation 2: [9] -> "Goldberg and West [ 9] 88 proved that a solution always exists if k= 2"
          Role: Proves a solution exists for k=2.

      Citation 3: [3] -> "Alon and West [ 3] gave a simpler proof using the Borsuk–Ulam theorem [ 7]"
          Role: Gives a simpler proof (using Borsuk-Ulam) for the necklace splitting problem.

      Citation 4: [7] -> "the Borsuk–Ulam theorem [ 7]"
          Role: The theorem used in the proof by Alon and West.

      Citation 5: [1] -> "Alon [ 1] generalized the results to k>2"
          Role: Generalizes the results to k>2.

      Citation 6: [2] -> "Alon and Graur [ 2] discovered an efficient approximation algorithm ... and also consider an online variation"
          Role: Discovered an efficient approximation algorithm and an online variation.

      Citation 7: [13] -> "Kash et al. [ 13] study a fair division problem with divisible goods ..."
          Role: Studies a fair division problem with divisible goods in dynamic settings.

      Citation 8: [5] -> "Benadeetal.[ 5] study the problem of allocating indivisible goods ..."
          Role: Studies allocating indivisible goods in online manner.

      Citation 9: [11] -> "He et al. [ 11] generalize this to a setting where reallocation is allowed but expensive."
          Role: Generalizes the previous work to allow reallocation (but expensive).

